.TH db "" "Command"
.SH NAME
db \- assembler-level symbolic debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdb [\-cdefkorst] [[\fImapfile\fB] \fIdatafile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBdb \-e \fIprogram\fB [\fIargument\fB ...]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
db -- assembler-level symbolic debugger
Usage:	db [ options ] [ [ mapfile ] datafile ]
By default, 'datafile' is assumed to be an object file.
'mapfile' defaults to 'l.out' and 'datafile' defaults to 'core'.
Options:
	-c	Map 'datafile' as a core file
	-d	Map 'datafile' as a system dump;
		'mapfile' defaults to '/coherent'
	-e	Next argument is object file and rest of command line
		is passed to the child process
	-f	Map 'datafile' as binary data
	-o	'datafile' is an object file
	-r	Access all files read-only
	-t	Perform input and output via /dev/tty
		rather than standard input and output
.HE
.B db
examines and patches object files, core dumps, system dumps and plain
files, and runs programs under
.B ptrace
control with breakpoints and single stepping.
It works at the machine level: it disassembles Z8001 instructions and
displays memory in the formats described below, but it reads the
symbol table of the object file so that addresses can be given and
printed by name.
Commands are read from the standard input.
.PP
.B db
sees a program as three
.IR "address spaces" :
the data space (\fBd\fR), the instruction space (\fBi\fR) and the
user area of the process (\fBu\fR), which holds the saved registers.
Each space is described by a list of address maps (see \fB:m\fR) that
tell where a range of addresses is to be found: in a file, or in a
running child process through
.BR ptrace .
What is mapped depends on the mode selected by the options.
.IP "\fB\-o\fR"
.I datafile
is an object file (the default when no option is given and one file is
named).
With two files,
.I mapfile
supplies the symbol table and
.I datafile
is the object that is examined and patched.
With no file,
.B l.out
is used.
.IP "\fB\-c\fR"
.I datafile
is a core file written by a user process.
Its user area and every dumped segment are mapped; pure segments and
the symbol table come from
.IR mapfile ,
which must be the object the process was running (\fBdb\fR complains
"Core different from object" otherwise).
With no file,
.B l.out
and
.B core
are used.
This is also the default when two files are named without an option.
.IP "\fB\-d\fR"
.I datafile
is a system dump;
.I mapfile
is the kernel.
With no file,
.B /coherent
and
.B /dev/dump
are used.
.IP "\fB\-k\fR"
Map
.I datafile
as a kernel image in memory: the whole file is the instruction space
and the user area, and the data space starts at the kernel's
.B etext_
symbol.
With no file,
.B /coherent
and
.B /dev/mem
are used, so a running system can be examined.
.IP "\fB\-f\fR"
Map
.I datafile
as a plain array of bytes, in both the data and the instruction space.
A
.I mapfile
may supply a symbol table.
.IP "\fB\-e\fR"
The next argument is an object file to be executed as a traced child
process; the remaining arguments are passed to it as its command line.
This lets the shell expand file names that a
.B :e
command would take literally.
.IP "\fB\-r\fR"
Open every file read-only.
Otherwise files are opened for writing when permitted (a note is
printed when a file could be opened read-only only), so that the
.B =
command can patch them.
.IP "\fB\-s\fR"
Do not load the symbol table.
.IP "\fB\-t\fR"
Do the debugger's own input and output on
.B /dev/tty
instead of the standard input and output, so that a program whose
standard streams are redirected can be debugged.
.SH "Expressions and Addresses"
Most commands take an address and a count, both written as
expressions.
An expression is built from
.IP "\fInumber\fR" 0.6i
An integer constant, hexadecimal by default.
A leading
.B 0
makes it octal, a leading
.B 0x
hexadecimal and a leading
.B #
decimal; the choice stays in effect for later constants.
.IP "\fB.\fR"
The current address, which every display command leaves after the
last item shown.
.IP "\fIsymbol\fR"
A name from the symbol table.
The name may be typed as the C compiler wrote it (\fBmain_\fR) or in
C form (\fBmain\fR); the first eight characters are significant.
The kind of a symbol decides the space of the expression: text symbols
name the instruction space, data and bss symbols the data space.
.IP "\fBd\fR, \fBi\fR, \fBu\fR"
Address 0 of the data space, the instruction space and the user area.
Adding to one of these selects that space explicitly.
.IP "\fBpc\fR, \fBr\fIn\fR, \fBrr\fIn\fR, \fBrh\fIn\fR, \fBrl\fIn\fR"
The registers of the traced process, as addresses in the user area, so
they can be displayed and patched like any other location.
.PP
The binary operators are
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.B *
and
.B /
with the usual precedence, the unary operators are
.B \-
(negation),
.B ~
(complement) and
.B *
(the word at the address), and parentheses group.
All arithmetic is done in longs.
An expression takes its space from its leftmost operand.
.PP
Where a command accepts several expressions they are separated by
commas.
An omitted address defaults to
.BR . ,
an omitted count to 1.
.SH "Display"
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB][,\fIcount\fB]?[\fIformat\fB]\fR" 0.6i
Display
.I count
items starting at
.IR address ,
each according to
.IR format .
The format string is remembered per space and is reused when omitted;
initially it is
.B w
for the data space and the user area and
.B i
for the instruction space.
Every item starts a line with its address and moves
.B .
past what it displayed.
The format is a sequence of the letters below, each optionally
preceded by a repeat count:
.RS
.IP \fBb\fR 0.4i
A byte.
.IP \fBw\fR
A word (16 bits);
.B h
is a synonym.
.IP \fBl\fR
A long (32 bits).
.IP \fBv\fR
A three-byte value, as stored in a file system.
.IP \fBc\fR
A character, with control characters and the backslash shown as C
escapes.
.IP \fBC\fR
A character, printed as is.
.IP \fBs\fR
A string ending in a null byte, escaped as for
.BR c .
.IP \fBS\fR
The same string without escapes.
.IP \fBf\fR
A single-precision floating point number.
.IP \fBF\fR
A double-precision floating point number.
.IP \fBi\fR
A machine instruction, disassembled.
.IP \fBp\fR
A long taken as an address, printed symbolically.
.IP \fBY\fR
A long taken as a time, printed as by
.BR date .
.IP "\fBd\fR, \fBo\fR, \fBu\fR, \fBx\fR"
Select decimal, octal, unsigned or hexadecimal for the numeric formats
that follow; hexadecimal is the default.
.IP "\fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR"
Move the address forward or back by the size of the last item without
displaying anything.
.IP \fB^\fR
Return to the address the command started at.
.IP \fBn\fR
Start a new output line.
.RE
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]=\fR"
Print
.I address
in hexadecimal.
.IP "\fIaddress\fB:a\fR"
Print
.I address
symbolically, as the nearest symbol below it plus an offset.
.IP "\fB!\fIcommand\fR"
Pass the rest of the line to the shell.
.IP "\fB?\fR"
Alone on a line, print the last error message.
Errors are otherwise reported by a single
.B ?
line.
.SH "Patching"
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]=\fIvalue\fB[,\fIvalue\fB...]\fR" 0.6i
Store the values (up to ten) at consecutive locations starting at
.IR address .
Each value has the size of the item last displayed, so a display
command normally precedes a patch.
The store goes to the mapped file, or to the traced process.
.IP "[\fIexpr\fB]:o\fR"
When
.I expr
is nonzero, swap the bytes of every word read from or written to the
target.
.SH "Running a Program"
A program is started under trace control with
.B :e
(or the
.B \-e
option), after which it can be run to breakpoints and single stepped.
The child stops at every breakpoint and every fault; the fault or the
breakpoint's commands are then run and
.B db
reads commands again.
.IP "\fB:e \fR[\fIargument\fB ...]\fR" 0.6i
Start the object file as a child process with the given arguments
(up to 19).
Quotes and backslashes are honoured;
.BR < ,
.B >
and
.B >>
redirect the child's standard input and output.
A child already running is killed first.
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]:b\fR[\fIcommands\fB]\fR"
Set a breakpoint at
.I address
and run
.I commands
whenever it is hit.
The default command string displays the address symbolically and
disassembles the instruction there, then waits for commands.
Up to 15 breakpoints may be set.
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]:br\fR[\fIcommands\fB]\fR"
Set a return breakpoint: it is taken when the function containing the
current pc returns to
.IR address .
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]:d\fR"
Delete the breakpoint at
.IR address ;
.B :dr
deletes a return breakpoint and
.B :ds
a step-over breakpoint, and
.B :da
deletes them all.
.IP "\fB:p\fR"
List the breakpoints.
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB]:c\fR"
Continue the child, at
.I address
if one is given.
.IP "[\fIaddress\fB][,\fIcount\fB]:s\fR[\fBc\fR][\fIcommands\fB]\fR"
Single step
.I count
instructions, starting at
.I address
if given, and run
.I commands
after each; by default the instruction at the new pc is disassembled.
With
.BR :sc ,
a
.B call
instruction is stepped over: the called function runs at full speed
and stepping resumes on return.
While stepping, an empty command line steps once more.
.IP "\fB:r\fR"
Display the registers of the stopped child.
.IP "[\fIdepth\fB]:t\fR"
Print a traceback of the child's stack, one line per active function
with the arguments it was called with, to
.I depth
levels (default: all).
.IP "\fB:f\fR"
Print the fault that stopped the child, or that produced the core file.
.IP "\fB:m\fR"
Print the address maps of the three spaces.
.IP "[\fIexpr\fB]:x\fR"
When
.I expr
is nonzero (the default), read commands from the standard input until
end of file or a
.B :q
with a zero argument.
Breakpoint command strings use this to stop and wait for the user.
.IP "[\fIexpr\fB]:q\fR"
When
.I expr
is nonzero (the default), kill the child if any and exit.
End of file on the standard input has the same effect.
.PP
An interrupt from the terminal stops a running display and is
reported as "Interrupt"; it does not kill the child.
.SH FILES
.TP 12
l.out
default object file
.TP
core
default core file
.TP
/coherent, /dev/dump, /dev/mem
defaults for
.B \-d
and
.B \-k
.TP
/lib/slibc.a
shared C library, mapped when the object uses it
.TP
/dev/tty
terminal used by
.B \-t
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cc, ld, nm, ptrace, core, l.out
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Most errors set the message printed by
.B ?
and print a single
.BR ? .
"Cannot single step" means that no child is being traced.
"Child process terminated (\fIn\fR)" reports the exit status of a child
that ran to completion.
"Ptrace error" reports a failure of the trace system call.
.SH NOTES
The breakpoint instruction is
.B sc #0x81
and single stepping uses the hardware single-step trap;
.B sc
and block-move instructions cannot be stepped that way, so
.B db
places a temporary breakpoint after them instead.
.PP
The
.B :k
command is accepted but sends no signal.
Registers live in the user area, so patching them takes
effect when the child next runs.
